<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-Portuguese Joint Declaration</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1987">1987</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-Portuguese Joint Declaration was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 13 April 1987 by Portugal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Following the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> agreement, it arranged the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on 20 December 1999 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-administrative-regions-of-china">Special Administrative Region</a>, with its own system guaranteed for fifty years, ending more than four centuries of Portuguese presence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-portuguese-joint-declaration.jpg" alt="Sino-Portuguese Joint Declaration" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the 16th century Portugal held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> on lease from successive Chinese governments for a symbolic annual rent of 500 tael. Although the territory remained under Chinese sovereignty, the Portuguese came to treat and govern it as a de facto colony. In 1887 Portugal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> signed the Sino-Portuguese Draft Minutes and the Sino-Portuguese Treaty of Peking, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> granted Portugal the perpetual occupation and government of Macau, and Portugal promised not to transfer it to another country without China's approval.</p><p>Colonial rule lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of 1974 brought to power in Portugal a democratic regime that sought to end colonialism. Talks between the two countries then settled that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> was Chinese territory under Portuguese administration, and the declaration set out the full framework for the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Its full title is the Joint Declaration of the Government of the Portuguese Republic and the Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> on the question of Macau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Under the principle of one country, two systems, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> was exempted from the socialist system and from several laws made by the central government. Its capitalist economy, legal system and liberal society were to remain unchanged for at least 50 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> would neither tax Macau nor make laws on its governance, and the region would have full autonomy in domestic affairs, economic policy and internal security, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> keeping control only of foreign affairs and defence.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the region kept the right to conclude agreements with Portugal and with international organisations for its own development. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a> was to enact a Basic Law setting out how the basic principles of Chinese government would apply there, and the declaration became the main source of the fundamental rights written into the Basic Law of the Macau Special Administrative Region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sino-Portuguese Joint Declaration” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Portugal's 1974 revolution, the 1987 <b>Joint Declaration</b> arranged its return, and on 20 December 1999 Macau became a Special Administrative Region of the People's Republic of China.</blockquote><a href="/state/macau-portuguese">Macau (Portuguese)</a></li></ul></section>
